const sections=[["A voice that asks to be heard","The poem begins with a gaucho claiming a place for his own song. Hernández builds a literary first-person narrator, not a documentary transcript. When Fierro describes his experiences, readers hear a compelling version of events—and must still consider what the narrator omits or excuses."],["Justice and the power of institutions","Forced frontier service, local authority, and the loss of home drive the conflict of the 1872 poem. Fierro presents himself as a victim of coercion. The historical background helps explain this complaint, but it does not turn every act he commits into a justifiable response."],["Family, displacement, and the longing to return","The breakdown of Fierro’s household gives emotional weight to his later flight. In the 1879 continuation, his sons speak of their own hardships. The poem's movement from separation to reunion is incomplete and troubled: returning does not restore the past exactly as it was."],["Friendship and the choice of Cruz","Sergeant Cruz makes a decisive choice when he refuses to leave Fierro alone against the armed party. Friendship becomes a counterweight to official force. Yet the alliance is forged in a violent scene, reminding readers that loyalty can be admirable without resolving every ethical question."],["Violence, racism, and the limits of the hero","Fierro's killing of an Afro-descendant man, the later encounter with the Moreno, and hostile depictions of Indigenous people demand a critical reading. The fact that a narrator speaks with emotional force does not guarantee his innocence or the accuracy of his judgments."],["The meaning of advice in the sequel","The second part gives greater prominence to testimony and counsel. The younger son's memories of Don Vizcacha's opportunistic sayings contrast with Fierro's later advice. Reading both voices exposes competing ways of surviving and living among others."],["Why the two parts should be read together","The first part, published in 1872, protests dispossession and authority. The second, published in 1879, returns to society and asks what comes after resistance. Neither part should erase the contradictions of the other. Their dialogue is at the center of the poem's lasting literary interest."]];
const sources=[{"name":"Original Spanish poem, canto I","url":"/leer/ida/1/"},{"name":"Original Spanish sequel","url":"/leer/vuelta/"},{"name":"English characters guide","url":"/en/characters/"},{"name":"Biblioteca Virtual Miguel de Cervantes: José Hernández","url":"https://www.cervantesvirtual.com/portales/jose_hernandez/"}];
